                        "entry_id": "daed705f-378e-3560-9602-b204912b139d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To seize or snatch up."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "96f8912e-c902-4a46-a8c3-ab64b5ecf17f",
                                "sense_id": "b76913e0-7189-3754-bd6b-9bed80c1c9ab",
                                "text": "With pity struck, with horror for my deed, The babe upsnatch'd away I bore with speed ; And, knowing Zion should be captive led, Far to these mountains of the East I sped."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8ca183a6-b2ac-425a-8754-0ad24203b560",
                                "sense_id": "b76913e0-7189-3754-bd6b-9bed80c1c9ab",
                                "text": "With Zephyr from his palace in the west, thou dost upsnatch the Twins from cradled rest, And strain them to thy breast, [...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e9f33c8a-16ea-4feb-9972-71452946f5e9",
                                "sense_id": "b76913e0-7189-3754-bd6b-9bed80c1c9ab",
                                "text": "To her the wood-ivy, like a spirit, hovers round the old hawthorn, and everywhere \"mystic Presences of power\" \"upsnatch\" her \"to the timeless, then return\" her \"to the Hour.\""
                            }
                        ]
                    }
